Urinary Incontinence (Stress/Urge)
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Leakage with exertion/coughing (Stress), Sudden uncontrollable desire to void (Urge), Frequent micturition/nocturia

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-046
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of severe, uncontrollable urinary incontinence resulting in a minor mechanical fall and acute psychological distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a sudden, complete loss of bladder control over the past 24 hours in an elderly patient, culminating in a slip-and-fall on a urine-soaked floor, alongside severe anxiety regarding loss of independence. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to evaluate for an acute urinary tract infection precipitating the flare, assess for injuries from the fall, and provide immediate continence management to prevent further falls before the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I couldn't make it to the bathroom in time, I completely soaked the floor, and I slipped and fell because I couldn't stop the flow.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a chronic history of mixed urinary incontinence, including involuntary leakage synchronous with physical exertion like coughing or laughing (stress incontinence), and a sudden, compelling, and uncontrollable desire to pass urine (urgency). Over the past 24 hours, her symptoms have acutely flared. She reports high frequency of micturition exceeding 15 times per day, severe nocturia (waking 6 times last night), and a total inability to reach the toilet in time following the sensation of an urge. This resulted in a fall today. She denies acute dysuria or hematuria but feels generally unwell. She reports leakage of large volumes rather than small drops today.
Functional Status: Ambulatory but moving stiffly due to the fall; deeply embarrassed and anxious.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of recurrent falls on wet surfaces; living alone.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/85 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 37.4 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Patient appears highly distressed, tearful, and is wearing heavily soiled clothing.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals no focal deficits; gait is steady but cautious. Musculoskeletal exam shows a superficial contusion on the right lateral hip from the fall, with no bony tenderness or shortening of the limb, suggesting no fracture. Genitourinary inspection reveals mild vulvar erythema from moisture. Point-of-care urinalysis demonstrates positive nitrites, moderate leukocytes, and trace blood, confirming an acute urinary tract infection as the likely trigger for the acute continence failure.
Environmental Observations: Puddle of urine on the bathroom tile; slip hazard identified.
Medication Reconciliation: No active bladder medications on file.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Urinary Tract Infection triggering severe Urge Incontinence exacerbation - Status: Acute flare with mechanical fall
Differentials:

Acute Neurological Event (e.g., Stroke/Cord Compression) - Ruled out by the intact neurological examination, normal bilateral lower limb power, and the clear presence of a severe localized bladder infection.

Hip Fracture - Ruled out clinically by the ability to straight-leg raise, bear weight, and the absence of bony tenderness over the greater trochanter or groin.

Overflow Incontinence (Urinary Retention) - Unlikely as the primary cause given the strong urge component and lack of a palpable, distended bladder, though a post-void residual would be ideal in a clinic setting.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Fall risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, catastrophic failure of bladder control (severe urge incontinence) that directly caused a mechanical fall. The objective clinical evidence (positive urinalysis) confirms that a new urinary tract infection is the primary driver of this acute exacerbation, severely irritating the detrusor muscle. The combination of a fall, the severe psychological distress regarding loss of independence, and the need to immediately initiate antibiotic therapy to calm the bladder spasms mandated urgent after-hours evaluation. Delayed assessment would have left the patient at extremely high risk for further falls on wet floors and worsening infection.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Cephalexin 500mg twice daily for 5 days for the acute urinary tract infection. Administered oral paracetamol 1g for the hip contusion pain. Assisted the patient with cleaning the bathroom floor to remove the immediate slip hazard. Provided a supply of high-capacity, absorbent incontinence pull-ups from the GP emergency kit.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent rushing and slipping when you feel an urge, immediately stop walking, cross your legs, and take three deep breaths before slowly proceeding to the bathroom; recognizing that pausing actually decreases the bladder spasm will reinforce this safer, slower approach. To reduce overnight bladder filling, completely stop all fluid intake two hours before bed; noting the reduction in nighttime waking will validate strict fluid timing.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a community continence nurse for home assessment.
DME/Safety (Optional): High-capacity incontinence pull-ups provided.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to check the hip contusion and ensure the UTI is resolving.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if you become completely unable to bear weight on your right leg, if you develop a high fever (over 38.5 C), or if you develop severe, sharp back pain.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The antibiotics will treat the infection causing this acute flare, but long-term continence management requires specialist assessment.
Clinical Course: The extreme, sudden urges to urinate should significantly decrease within 48 hours of starting the antibiotics. The hip bruise will be sore for several days.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if you cannot walk due to hip pain, or if you develop a high fever and shaking chills.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You have developed a severe bladder infection, which has severely irritated the muscles of your bladder, causing them to spasm violently and empty without warning. This is why you suddenly lost all control today. The antibiotics will kill the bacteria and stop the spasms. The bruise on your hip is just a soft-tissue injury, but you must wear the absorbent pull-ups provided tonight so you do not have to rush to the bathroom and risk another fall.
Actionable Steps: Start the antibiotics immediately. Wear the pull-ups to prevent rushing. Use the pause-and-breathe technique when you feel an urge, and see your GP in three days.

